WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.660
Stealing from "Star Wars." Ignoring The Wasp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.700
MODOK butt! Fans are seriously frustrated&nbsp;
with "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania."

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.240
The first big issue fans took&nbsp;
with "Ant-Man and the Wasp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.260
Quantumania" is the lack of Quantum&nbsp;
Realm rules. The entire premise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.820
the Quantum Realm is that it is an unknown&nbsp;
location seemingly ruled by chaos. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.680
the film tries to reflect this by letting a bunch&nbsp;
of inexplicable things happen and not giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.840
audience any sort of explanation for the events.&nbsp;
While a few things were explained — like how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.980
"ooze" let the characters understand every&nbsp;
language if consumed — that just left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.880
other aspects of the Quantum journey that&nbsp;
were not even touched on even more confusing.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.920
For example, how could humans breathe air in a&nbsp;
subatomic atmosphere? It makes sense that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.700
creatures who live down there have adapted to&nbsp;
their surroundings, but when Scott and company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.560
head to the Quantum Realm, they can all breathe&nbsp;
the air without their suits. One fan on Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.660
even questioned how breathing was even possible if&nbsp;
the Quantum Realm is smaller than air molecules.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.020
"This isn't exactly ant science, is it?"

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.760
One of the biggest gags of the film is the&nbsp;
introduction of MODOK. The man behind MODOK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.840
turns out to be a face that "Ant-Man" fans&nbsp;
will recognize from the franchise — Darren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.940
played by Corey Stoll. Making Darren the person&nbsp;
inside the mechanism is a decent enough idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.280
especially since Marvel films love to bring&nbsp;
back familiar faces. However, the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.320
ultimately ends up being little more than a big&nbsp;
joke and not much of a threat at all. What's more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.440
the CGI for MODOK is incredibly bad, to the point&nbsp;
of being distracting. One fan on Twitter said,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.660
"There's LOTS of questionable CGI in this&nbsp;
one. MODOK in particular looked atrocious."

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.860
Though the character is definitely supposed&nbsp;
to be hard to look at, there is no scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.720
in which he is supposed to be this ridiculous.&nbsp;
The character's visual representation makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.640
impossible to view him as a threat, too — even&nbsp;
more so because of the random, unnecessary nude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.440
shot the audience is surprised by when he is&nbsp;
fastened into his machinery. One fan asked,

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.380
"What did I do to deserve&nbsp;
seeing MODOK butt naked?"

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.640
MODOK is good for a few laughs but&nbsp;
is otherwise mostly a frustrating and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.960
forgettable character because of the&nbsp;
poor CGI and the unnecessary nudity.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.360
"It's a hole so deep, it cannot be filled."

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.220
When Scott and his cohorts are pulled into the&nbsp;
Quantum Realm, they immediately get separated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.620
This leaves Scott and Cassie searching for&nbsp;
Hank, Janet, and Hope. Having spent 30 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.880
in the Quantum Realm, Janet knows the most out&nbsp;
of all of them and is their best bet to navigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.380
their way through the chaotic environment.&nbsp;
Janet leads Hank and Hope to a bar of sorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.300
and fans of the "Star Wars" franchise may feel&nbsp;
familiar with this setting — especially because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.680
of its distinct similarities to the Mos&nbsp;
Eisley cantina in "Star Wars: A New Hope."

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.240
In "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania,"&nbsp;
creatures of all kinds are seen drinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.200
together at the bar — just like in "A New&nbsp;
Hope." This led some fans to believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.620
the bar scene was further proof that&nbsp;
"Quantumania" was trying a little too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.180
hard to be like "Star Wars." One Twitter user&nbsp;
was upset about the similarities and tweeted,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.640
"Why was Quantumania just a weird Star Wars&nbsp;
rip off?!?! MCU baby, what is you doing"

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.920
Another Twitter user noticed some similarities&nbsp;
with another popular MCU film, writing,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.460
"Quantum realm is basically star&nbsp;
wars x guardians of the galaxy."

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.380
Though the creatures are not the same, and there&nbsp;
are certainly some unique elements, the energized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.060
music and overall setting were too similar&nbsp;
to the famous cantina scene for many viewers.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.040
One consistent theme throughout all of the&nbsp;
"Ant-Man" films is Scott's love for his daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.000
Cassie. Their relationship is Scott's&nbsp;
motivation for everything he does in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.380
his life, and Scott does not hesitate to put&nbsp;
himself in harm's way if it means ensuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.220
Cassie's safety. This is no different&nbsp;
in "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania."

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.960
When Cassie and Scott are captured by Kang,&nbsp;
Scott is asked to use the Pym Particles to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.100
fix the energy source for Kang's ship so&nbsp;
he can escape the Quantum Realm. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.200
Scott refuses to do this because he does not want&nbsp;
to help Kang and act against Janet's instructions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.720
Kang tries to torture Scott to get him to change&nbsp;
his mind, but he does not budge. His opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.860
quickly changes, though, when Kang begins&nbsp;
to torture Cassie and threatens to kill her.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.920
Kang torturing Cassie, though&nbsp;
not totally unexpected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.820
is a low blow. Nothing in the world is&nbsp;
more important to Scott than Cassie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.140
which is made clear by the scene where all of&nbsp;
the Scott possibilities are working together to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.640
acquire the energy source only in an effort&nbsp;
to save Cassie. A fan wrote on Twitter,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.440
"The 'for Cassie' scene with Scott's probability&nbsp;
variants forming an anthill had me bawling."

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.220
Fans agree that Scott's love for Cassie&nbsp;
is the best part of the "Ant-Man" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.760
making the fact that he has to watch&nbsp;
her in pain all the more upsetting.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.860
Perhaps the most frustrating part of "Ant-Man&nbsp;
and the Wasp: Quantumania" is the anticlimactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.140
third act, which finally showcases Kang's power.&nbsp;
Throughout the film, Kang is portrayed as this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.360
ominously powerful being. He can kill on command&nbsp;
and has seemingly unlimited strength that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.920
from his helmet and suit. Beyond that, he does not&nbsp;
hesitate to show this power off when he wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.720
get his way. It makes sense that when Kang fights&nbsp;
Scott the two are evenly matched — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:57.180
Kang's suit is damaged and Scott's suit is still&nbsp;
intact. However, before Kang's suit is broken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.680
it is ultimately the ants and MODOK that&nbsp;
take him down, which seems a bit silly.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.260
The ants were pretty advanced, as Hank explains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.460
but it doesn't really seem like that — even&nbsp;
combined with MODOK's sneak attack — would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.360
be even remotely enough to beat the man&nbsp;
who has killed several Avengers. The way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.920
that Kang is ultimately defeated ends up feeling&nbsp;
more like a cop-out than a well-deserved victory.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.320
At the end of the film, Janet is able to open the&nbsp;
portal to get everyone home, but they have to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.480
quickly. Hank and Janet jump through, followed&nbsp;
by Hope. However, Scott pushes Cassie through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:32.760
but stays back to keep Kang from getting out,&nbsp;
leading to an intense battle. When it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.680
all hope is lost for Scott making it back, Hope&nbsp;
crashes through the portal at the last second and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.540
helps Scott take down Kang. However, the portal&nbsp;
closes, and Scott and Hope are left in the Quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:48.180
Realm…for all of two seconds, until Cassie reopens&nbsp;
the portal, allowing Hope and Scott to escape.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:53.700
The situation seems incredibly anticlimactic,&nbsp;
with no stakes or consequences that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.780
any impact on the characters. They defeat&nbsp;
Kang, but instead of getting stuck there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.020
giving Cassie and her grandparents a&nbsp;
chance to try to get them out over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.620
they are immediately able to&nbsp;
make it home. One fan tweeted,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:09.180
"The movie would've felt more consequential if&nbsp;
Hope/Scott were stuck in the quantum realm."

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.220
It makes sense how Cassie was able to quickly&nbsp;
open the portal, but the pacing felt off. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.360
a brief scene of Cassie panicking and trying&nbsp;
to reconnect the device would have added some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:23.220
needed drama to the situation. For many fans, to&nbsp;
have Hope and Scott's sacrifice mean virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.900
nothing, as they are saved immediately, is&nbsp;
frustrating and narratively unsatisfying.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.440
"Easy-peasy."
 
One of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.180
criticisms comes from the film's lack of Hope&nbsp;
content. There is a lot of room made for Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.740
Rudd's Scott Lang to tap into the plot and his&nbsp;
emotional depth, while Jonathan Majors' Kang the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:45.720
Conquerer steals the show. Michelle Pfeiffer dives&nbsp;
into her role as Janet as she guides the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:51.000
through the Quantum Realm. However, Hope, played&nbsp;
by actress Evangeline Lilly, is shoved to the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.280
in favor of these other characters. Fans found&nbsp;
this pretty disappointing — especially since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.320
film is supposed to be "Ant-Man and the Wasp," not&nbsp;
just "Ant-Man." One fan on Twitter said it best,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:07.380
"Evangeline Lilly as Wasp in #Quantumania felt&nbsp;
nonexistent or maybe I can articulate it by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.560
saying she was there physically&nbsp;
yet her presence wasn't felt."

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.880
Another fan theorized on Twitter that Lilly&nbsp;
might've been shoved to the sidelines because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.800
of her vaccination views, which she publicly&nbsp;
talked about during the coronavirus pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:24.240
However, as the same user points out, Letitia&nbsp;
Wright, who took the lead in "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.680
Wakanda Forever," has similar views and&nbsp;
is in nearly every scene of her film.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:32.760
No matter what the reason, it is clear&nbsp;
that the Wasp is much less of a priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.080
in this film and seemingly in the MCU&nbsp;
in general. Lilly has a lot of talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.280
and brings a lot to the scenes she is in,&nbsp;
but she is not in nearly as many as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.040
should be for it to be considered&nbsp;
an "Ant-Man and the Wasp" film.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.420
Though this is not a specific moment from&nbsp;
the film, we would be remiss if we did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:53.340
mention how upset fans were upon the recasting&nbsp;
of Cassie. Everyone knew young Cassie from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.960
first couple of films would be recast because of&nbsp;
the blip, but Kathryn Newton was not supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.700
be Cassie. The role was supposed to be played by&nbsp;
Emma Fuhrmann, a young actress who played Cassie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.500
for a brief scene in "Avengers: Endgame." Her&nbsp;
emotional depth and resemblance to Rudd's Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.660
Lang were evident from just a short scene with&nbsp;
the two of them — so why did Newton play Cassie?

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:18.360
When Fuhrmann was cast, Cassie's role was just a&nbsp;
small, bit part for one film. Now that Cassie has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.740
been given a more important role in the MCU,&nbsp;
the casting team at Marvel opted for someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.960
with more experience and a more recognizable&nbsp;
name. Unfortunately, Fuhrmann was not told of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.400
this change directly, and many fans weren't&nbsp;
happy. In a Twitter post, one fan wrote,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.100
"Emma Furhmann, Cassie's actress from Endgame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.060
was robbed of the chance of a lifetime&nbsp;
and found out about it the exact same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.680
we all did – through the announcement on&nbsp;
social media. They didn't even tell her."

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.880
The way it was handled is devastating&nbsp;
and leaves fans wondering if Fuhrmann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.740
would have been the better choice for the role.

